                <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
                <div class="mt-16">
                  <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
                    Common Mistakes When Choosing a Concrete Sealer
                  </h2>

                  <p>
                    Concrete sealer is really two different product families that happen to share a shelf. Choosing from the wrong family is the mistake that leads to most of the others.
                  </p>

                  <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Matching Sealer Type to the Job</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Penetrating silanes and siloxanes soak in, change nothing about the appearance, and last years — ideal for driveways, patios, and anywhere freeze-thaw matters. Acrylics and epoxies form a film that enhances colour and resists stains but wears and needs re-coating. Buying one when you needed the other is the root of most disappointment.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Concrete That Has Not Cured</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        New concrete needs about 28 days before it is ready to seal. Applied earlier, the moisture still leaving the slab clouds a film sealer and prevents a penetrating one from soaking in properly. There is no way to hurry this and no way to fix it afterwards short of stripping.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying in the Wrong Conditions</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Most sealers want a surface between roughly 50°F and 90°F, out of direct midday sun, with no rain for 24 hours afterwards. Applied to a hot slab, solvent flashes off before the sealer can penetrate; applied cold or damp, it never cures properly. Early morning or evening on a mild dry day is the window.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Sealer Fixes Existing Damage</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Sealer is preventative. It will not close cracks, lift stains, or restore a spalled surface — it will lock all of that in and make it permanent. Repair cracks, remove oil, and address any spalling before sealing, or you are sealing the damage in place.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Closing -->
                <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
                  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                    The best <strong class="text-white">concrete sealer</strong> is decided by what the slab has to survive. Penetrating silane or siloxane sealers are the right default for driveways, walkways, and anything exposed to freeze-thaw, because they protect from within and never peel. Acrylics suit decorative and stamped work where colour and sheen matter, and epoxies or polyaspartics belong on garage and workshop floors that take chemical and abrasion abuse.
                  </p>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
                    Get the timing right and any of them will perform: a fully cured slab, a clean and repaired surface, a mild dry day, and thin even coats. Seal the wrong slab, or the right slab too early, and no product on the shelf will save the result.
                  </p>
                </div>

